&lt;td&gt;$9,200 ($767)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5,000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$1,125&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$0.225&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$11,250 ($938)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7,500&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$1,688&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$0.225&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$16,880 ($1,407)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10,000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$2,250&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$0.225&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$22,500 ($1,875)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What the table tells you:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Up to 800 credits there's no volume discount.&lt;/strong&gt; Every tier is $0.25 a credit. Pick the smallest tier that covers your month.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Annual billing is the real discount.&lt;/strong&gt; Lovable says it's two months free: $250 instead of 12 × $25 = $300.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Top-ups cost the same as the base plan.&lt;/strong&gt; Lovable's docs list the Pro top-up rate at $0.25 per credit ($10 buys 40 credits). Running out on the 100-credit tier and topping up costs the same per credit as moving to 200.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pro also gives you:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;5 daily build credits with no monthly cap.&lt;/strong&gt; If you build every day, that's up to about 150 extra credits in a 30-day month. They expire at the end of each day (00:00 UTC), so they only help on days you actually build.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rollover.&lt;/strong&gt; Unused monthly credits roll over while your subscription is active. On monthly billing they expire 2 months after they were issued.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Code editing, code download, custom domains, and removing the "Edit with Lovable" badge.&lt;/strong&gt; These are listed as Pro and above in the plan comparison.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Business and Enterprise
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Business&lt;/strong&gt; starts at &lt;strong&gt;$50 a month for 100 credits&lt;/strong&gt; ($500 a year), up to $4,300 a month for 10,000. That's double the Pro price for the same credits, and the docs list the Business top-up rate at $0.50 a credit. You're paying for team controls and governance, not more building.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Enterprise&lt;/strong&gt; is a sales conversation. No price is published.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Student and teacher discount
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Verified students and teachers get &lt;strong&gt;50% off the 100-credit Pro plan, billed monthly, for up to 12 months&lt;/strong&gt;. Lovable checks your school email domain, and you have to verify from a Free workspace. Higher tiers, annual billing and top-ups aren't discounted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Two things to know before you budget
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Your live app spends the same credits.&lt;/strong&gt; Hosting, the database and AI features in your deployed app draw on the same balance once the small monthly grants run out.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Chat pricing is temporary.&lt;/strong&gt; The docs say the current chat pricing, including the free daily chat allowance, applies through &lt;strong&gt;31 October 2026&lt;/strong&gt; and may change after that.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cheaper options to compare
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Base44:&lt;/strong&gt; Starter is $20/mo, or $16/mo on yearly billing. Free plan available.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Emergent:&lt;/strong&gt; Standard is $20/mo, or $17/mo on yearly billing. Free plan includes 10 credits a month.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Blink:&lt;/strong&gt; Starter is $25/mo, or $13/mo on yearly billing ($150 a year). Free plan available.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each vendor defines a "credit" differently, so compare what one credit actually does before you compare prices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Checklist before you upgrade
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Stay on Free until you've hit the 30-credit cap at least once.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Track a normal week of building and multiply by four.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Start on Pro 100. If you run out, a top-up costs the same per credit as the next tier.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If you'll stick with it for a year, annual saves two months.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Students and teachers: verify for the discount &lt;em&gt;before&lt;/em&gt; you upgrade, since verification needs a Free workspace.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Recheck docs.lovable.dev on the day you buy. Credit tiers change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I track Lovable's plans, with source links and check dates, on &lt;a href="https://buildstacktools.com/pricing/lovable.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Build Stack Tools&lt;/a&gt;, a free directory of AI app builders and coding tools. It also has &lt;a href="https://buildstacktools.com/alternatives/lovable.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Lovable alternatives&lt;/a&gt; if you're shopping around.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Short answer:&lt;/strong&gt; For debugging cron trigger issues in scheduled webhook delivery, verify the schedule and public endpoint first, trigger one run, then inspect run history before changing retries. For a media shipment fan-out, use cron to enqueue work and let an idempotent Node.js worker deliver the update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A media shipment update is a bad place to debug cron by staring at the expression. The useful question is operational: did the schedule fire, did the public endpoint answer during that window, and can I prove what happened after a retry?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with evidence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My choice for this shape of system is a cron trigger that starts queue work, followed by a worker that fans out the update. A plain REST API such as Infrai fits when I want one key and no SDK installation, while AWS EventBridge Scheduler with SQS, Google Cloud Scheduler with Pub/Sub, or Temporal can be better when their surrounding platform is already the center of gravity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The constraint changes the design. A cron execution has a 900-second ceiling, so the cron task should hand off quickly. The worker owns the fan-out. Standard queues are at-least-once, which means the subscriber update needs an idempotency key derived from the shipment event, not from the delivery attempt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How do I debug cron trigger issues for scheduled webhook delivery?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with the schedule configuration. Check that its expression and timezone describe the shipment window you actually need. Then check the endpoint: it must be a public HTTP URL for the cron task, and a push subscription target must be public HTTPS. An internal hostname can make a correct schedule look broken.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next, inspect run history. A missing run points at configuration or scheduling. A run with an error points at endpoint availability, response handling, or payload validation. A successful run followed by missing subscriber updates points downstream, where the queue and worker need attention. This split is more useful than adding retries to every layer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, suppose the shipment schedule should notify subscribers at 09:00 but the endpoint logs nothing. I would first inspect the run list for that cron ID, then manually trigger the same task while watching the public endpoint, and only after that inspect the queue worker. If the manual trigger produces a run but no HTTP response, the endpoint boundary is the suspect. If it produces a response and the worker later repeats the event, the fix belongs in consumer idempotency. This sequence preserves the distinction between “the timer did not fire” and “the timer fired but the delivery path was unhealthy,” which are different incidents with different owners and different rollback decisions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The manual trigger is the fastest way to separate those cases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pause and resume are useful isolation tools, too. Pausing does not backfill missed triggers, so record the window you are testing before changing state. Run history output is retained only for the first 4 KB, and trigger timing has second-level jitter; neither is a full distributed trace. My rule is to change one boundary at a time: schedule, endpoint, queue, then subscriber.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Can a Node.js check connect runs, dead letters, and errors?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes, if the operational surface stays small. The example below uses the same &lt;code&gt;Authorization&lt;/code&gt; header and base URL for scheduling and observability. It triggers one existing cron task, reads its run history, then reads captured error groups. The returned scheduling result is carried into the observation object so the worker can attach the run identifier to its own alert context without inventing a second credential path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is deliberately a diagnostic slice, not a vendor SDK. There are only two write calls: the manual trigger and, in a real worker, the subscriber delivery. Both need idempotency. The API route names here are limited to the verified scheduling and error surfaces.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Json&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[];&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cronId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;CRON_ID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cronId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY and CRON_ID are required&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;INFRAI_BASE_URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;INFRAI_BASE_URL is required&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;GET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;POST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;...(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Idempotency-Key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{})&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;429&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;retry-after&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;as &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; failed: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;inspectShipmentFanout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;():&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;triggerPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/cron/trigger/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encodeURIComponent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cronId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;runsPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/cron/runs/list/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encodeURIComponent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cronId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;trigger&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;triggerPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;POST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`shipment-debug-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cronId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;runs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;runsPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;GET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/errors/groups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;GET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;observation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;trigger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;runs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;shipment-cron&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;observation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;inspectShipmentFanout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The retry is intentionally conservative. It honors &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt;, but it is still bounded by the operational runner that invokes this check; production code should add a maximum attempt count and a jittered exponential delay. The trigger has an idempotency key because a network timeout after the server accepted the request is not proof that nothing ran.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A worker should consume the resulting shipment event and use an event-level key such as &lt;code&gt;shipmentId:statusVersion&lt;/code&gt; when updating subscribers. FIFO deduplication is only five minutes. Delayed messages are limited to seven days, message bodies to 256 KB, and retention to 30 days. Those are design limits, not knobs to hide an unbounded backlog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How do the real alternatives change recovery work?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Integration&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Best fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Main recovery limit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;EventBridge Scheduler + SQS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Managed AWS services&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An existing AWS estate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;IAM, scheduler, queue, and logs need correlation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cloud Scheduler + Pub/Sub&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Managed Google Cloud services&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A Google Cloud estate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Recovery is split across scheduler and subscription state&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Temporal&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Workflow engine and worker SDKs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Multi-step durable workflows&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Extra machinery for one HTTP handoff&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;BullMQ&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Node.js library plus Redis&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A Redis-backed Node.js service&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You own scheduler and observability glue&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One REST scheduling surface&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;HTTP calls from any language&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Small cron-to-queue recovery path&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One vendor and one outage surface&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS EventBridge Scheduler plus SQS gives a familiar managed pairing. Scheduler handles time; SQS gives queueing and a dead-letter queue. The trade-off is operational stitching: two AWS resource types, IAM policy work, CloudWatch views, and application glue to correlate a scheduled invocation with a queue message. It is the sensible choice for a team already standardized on AWS and willing to keep that vocabulary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google Cloud Scheduler plus Pub/Sub has a similar shape. It works well inside a Google Cloud estate, with IAM, logging, and retry policy living in that ecosystem. The recovery path is still spread across scheduler history, topic or subscription state, and the consumer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Temporal is a different category. It is a workflow engine, not just a cron-to-webhook handoff. Its durable workflow history and orchestration model are valuable when the shipment process grows into timers, compensation, and multi-step state. They are extra machinery for one scheduled HTTP delivery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;BullMQ is the pragmatic Node.js option when I already run Redis and want queue primitives in application code. It gives a developer-friendly worker model, but I still own the scheduler, endpoint exposure, observability, and recovery boundaries around it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The one-API approach reduces glue for this narrow case: one REST key, a cron route, queue work, run history, and captured errors can share the same request context. Its cost is real. I am trusting one vendor, one bill, and one outage surface. It also has no DAG or workflow orchestration, no fan-out join primitive, no native debounce or throttle, and no topic-style one-to-many delivery; multiple queues are required to simulate that last pattern.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I would change at scale
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would keep the cron task short and put a worker boundary after it. The worker would persist a shipment event identifier before sending subscriber updates, acknowledge only after its idempotent work completes, and send poison messages to a dead-letter path for inspection. I would also export the run identifier into the worker log context.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would not use cron as a seven-day scheduler, a workflow engine, or a replay system. A paused task does not replay missed executions. Acknowledged messages are deleted, so this is not Kafka-style replay with multiple consumer groups. If the business needs those semantics, Temporal, Pub/Sub, SQS, or a log-based platform deserves the lead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The decision rule is short: use the smallest scheduler-plus-worker design that makes a failed shipment update explainable. Validate the endpoint, trigger one run, read the run history, and correlate the worker's error record. The tool matters less than preserving that recovery path.&lt;/p&gt;

