&lt;h2&gt;


  AWS Deprecates Python 3.10 for Lambda: The Urgent Need to Migrate to Python 3.12+
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you’re running Python 3.10 on AWS Lambda, mark your calendar: &lt;strong&gt;October 31, 2026&lt;/strong&gt; is the deprecation deadline. After this date, your Lambda functions will continue to run, but they’ll lose critical security patches, become uneditable via the console, and face creation and update blocks by &lt;strong&gt;July 29, 2027&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;August 31, 2027&lt;/strong&gt;, respectively. This isn’t just a nudge—it’s a hard stop that jeopardizes application stability and security. The clock is ticking, and migrating to Python 3.12+ isn’t optional; it’s mandatory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Why Python 3.10 is Being Deprecated: The Technical Underpinnings
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS’s decision to deprecate Python 3.10 aligns with Python’s own end-of-life (EOL) timeline and AWS’s push for modern, secure standards. Python 3.10 reached EOL in October 2023, meaning no more security patches or updates. AWS can’t sustain runtimes for versions Python no longer supports. Here’s the causal chain: &lt;strong&gt;EOL → no upstream patches → AWS drops support → security risks for users.&lt;/strong&gt; It’s a mechanical process driven by dependency chains, not a whim.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The Real Work: Migrating to Python 3.12+
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Upgrading from Python 3.10 to 3.12+ isn’t just a version bump—it’s a structural overhaul. Here’s what breaks and why:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;distutils Removal (Python 3.12):&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;distutils&lt;/em&gt;, a legacy package management tool, is gone in 3.12. Any code importing it—old &lt;em&gt;setup.py&lt;/em&gt; scripts, outdated libraries—will fail. &lt;strong&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/strong&gt; Python 3.12’s interpreter no longer recognizes the &lt;em&gt;distutils&lt;/em&gt; module, triggering &lt;em&gt;ModuleNotFoundError&lt;/em&gt;. &lt;strong&gt;Solution:&lt;/strong&gt; Replace &lt;em&gt;distutils&lt;/em&gt; with &lt;em&gt;setuptools&lt;/em&gt; or &lt;em&gt;packaging&lt;/em&gt;. Rule: &lt;em&gt;If X uses distutils → use setuptools/packaging.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Removed Modules in 3.13 (PEP 594):&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;cgi&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;crypt&lt;/em&gt;, and &lt;em&gt;telnetlib&lt;/em&gt; are deprecated in 3.13. &lt;strong&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/strong&gt; These modules are flagged for removal to reduce Python’s maintenance burden. Code relying on them will fail at runtime. &lt;strong&gt;Solution:&lt;/strong&gt; Rewrite or find alternatives. Rule: &lt;em&gt;If X uses cgi/crypt/telnetlib → refactor or replace.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;OS Shift: Amazon Linux 2023:&lt;/strong&gt; Python 3.12+ on Lambda runs on Amazon Linux 2023, not AL2. &lt;strong&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/strong&gt; Compiled wheels and layers (e.g., &lt;em&gt;numpy&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;psycopg2&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;cryptography&lt;/em&gt;) built for AL2 are incompatible with AL2023’s glibc version. &lt;strong&gt;Observable effect:&lt;/strong&gt; ImportError or segmentation faults. &lt;strong&gt;Solution:&lt;/strong&gt; Rebuild wheels/layers for AL2023. Rule: &lt;em&gt;If X uses compiled dependencies → rebuild for AL2023.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Edge Cases and Common Pitfalls
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not all migrations are smooth. Here are edge cases and typical errors:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Python 3.11 as a Stopgap:&lt;/strong&gt; Python 3.11 is still on AL2 and deprecated on &lt;strong&gt;June 30, 2027&lt;/strong&gt;. It’s a short-term fix, not a solution. &lt;strong&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/strong&gt; 3.11 buys time but inherits AL2’s eventual EOL. &lt;strong&gt;Optimal choice:&lt;/strong&gt; Skip 3.11 and go straight to 3.12+. Rule: &lt;em&gt;If X needs immediate migration → use 3.12+, not 3.11.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hidden Dependencies:&lt;/strong&gt; Libraries with indirect &lt;em&gt;distutils&lt;/em&gt; dependencies (e.g., &lt;em&gt;scikit-learn&lt;/em&gt; &amp;lt; 1.3) will fail. &lt;strong&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/strong&gt; Transitive dependencies pull in &lt;em&gt;distutils&lt;/em&gt;, breaking the import chain. &lt;strong&gt;Solution:&lt;/strong&gt; Pin or update dependencies. Rule: &lt;em&gt;If X has transitive distutils → update dependencies.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Practical Migration Steps
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To identify affected functions, use this AWS CLI command:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws lambda list-functions &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--function-version&lt;/span&gt; ALL &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--query&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Functions[?Runtime=='python3.10'].FunctionName,Version"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--output&lt;/span&gt; text
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For a detailed migration guide and AWS source links, check the &lt;a href="https://eolwatch.pages.dev/guides/lambda-python-3-10-deprecation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;free AWS end-of-support tracker&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Conclusion: Act Now, Avoid Chaos
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The deprecation of Python 3.10 isn’t just a technical footnote—it’s a hard deadline with real consequences. Migrating to Python 3.12+ requires addressing removed modules, OS changes, and dependency updates. Procrastination risks security breaches, operational disruptions, and last-minute scrambles. &lt;strong&gt;Rule of thumb:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;If X runs on Python 3.10 → migrate to 3.12+ before October 2026.&lt;/em&gt; The mechanism is clear: AWS drops support → functions become vulnerable → applications fail. Don’t let your Lambda functions become collateral damage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Impact Analysis: AWS Deprecates Python 3.10 for Lambda
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS’s deprecation of Python 3.10 for Lambda functions isn’t just a calendar event—it’s a mechanical trigger for a cascade of failures. Here’s the breakdown of what breaks, why it breaks, and how it impacts your operations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Loss of Security Patches: The Silent Decay
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting October 31, 2026, Python 3.10 Lambda functions stop receiving security patches. This isn’t a theoretical risk—it’s a physical process of vulnerability accumulation. Without upstream patches from Python’s maintainers, every unaddressed CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures) becomes a permanent crack in your runtime. Attackers exploit these cracks through mechanisms like buffer overflows, injection attacks, or privilege escalations. The causal chain is clear: &lt;strong&gt;No patches → Unpatched CVEs → Exploitable vulnerabilities → Compromised functions.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Console Edit Block: The Operational Handcuff
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Post-deprecation, the AWS console disables edits for Python 3.10 functions. This isn’t just an inconvenience—it’s a mechanical block in your CI/CD pipeline. The console’s backend API rejects PUT requests for these functions, returning a &lt;em&gt;400 Bad Request&lt;/em&gt; with a deprecation error. The impact? &lt;strong&gt;Hotfixes delayed → Incident resolution slowed → Operational downtime.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Creation/Update Blocks: The Hard Stop
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By July 29, 2027, AWS blocks creation of new Python 3.10 functions. On August 31, 2027, updates to existing ones are blocked. This is a physical gate implemented in AWS’s Lambda control plane. The API endpoint for function creation/update (&lt;em&gt;CreateFunction&lt;/em&gt;/&lt;em&gt;UpdateFunctionConfiguration&lt;/em&gt;) returns a &lt;em&gt;403 Forbidden&lt;/em&gt; for Python 3.10. The causal chain: &lt;strong&gt;API block → Deployment pipeline halted → Application stagnation.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Technical Breakages in Python 3.12+: The Hidden Landmines
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  a. &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; Removal: The Dependency Domino
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Python 3.12 removes &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt;, replaced by &lt;code&gt;setuptools&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;packaging&lt;/code&gt;. Any code importing &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; fails with a &lt;em&gt;ModuleNotFoundError&lt;/em&gt;. The mechanism: Python’s interpreter no longer includes the &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; module in its standard library. The impact: &lt;strong&gt;Legacy &lt;code&gt;setup.py&lt;/code&gt; scripts break → Build processes fail → Deployment blocked.&lt;/strong&gt; Solution: Replace &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;setuptools&lt;/code&gt; or refactor build scripts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  b. Removed Modules (&lt;code&gt;cgi&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;crypt&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;telnetlib&lt;/code&gt;): The Dead Batteries
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Python 3.13 removes these modules per PEP 594. Code importing them fails at runtime with a &lt;em&gt;ModuleNotFoundError&lt;/em&gt;. The mechanism: Python’s interpreter explicitly excludes these modules. The impact: &lt;strong&gt;Legacy code relying on these modules crashes → Functionality loss.&lt;/strong&gt; Solution: Refactor to use modern alternatives (e.g., &lt;code&gt;hashlib&lt;/code&gt; instead of &lt;code&gt;crypt&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  c. OS Shift to Amazon Linux 2023: The Binary Incompatibility
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Python 3.12+ on Lambda runs on Amazon Linux 2023 (AL2023), not AL2. Compiled wheels/layers (e.g., &lt;code&gt;numpy&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;psycopg2&lt;/code&gt;) built for AL2’s glibc version fail on AL2023. The mechanism: AL2023 uses a newer glibc, causing symbol resolution errors or segmentation faults. The impact: &lt;strong&gt;Compiled extensions break → Functions crash at runtime.&lt;/strong&gt; Solution: Rebuild wheels/layers for AL2023.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  5. Edge Cases: Python 3.11 as a Temporary Crutch
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Python 3.11 is deprecated on June 30, 2027, and remains on AL2. It’s a short-lived stopgap. The optimal choice is migrating directly to 3.12+ because:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3.11’s AL2 compatibility is temporary.&lt;/strong&gt; You’ll face the same OS shift later.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3.12+ aligns with Python’s long-term support (LTS) strategy.&lt;/strong&gt; It avoids double migrations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rule of thumb: &lt;strong&gt;If using Python 3.10 → migrate directly to 3.12+ before October 2026.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  6. Hidden Dependencies: The Transitive Trap
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Libraries with transitive &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; dependencies (e.g., &lt;code&gt;scikit-learn &amp;lt; 1.3&lt;/code&gt;) break in Python 3.12+. The mechanism: These libraries import &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; internally, triggering a &lt;em&gt;ModuleNotFoundError&lt;/em&gt;. The impact: &lt;strong&gt;Seemingly unrelated libraries fail → Deployment blocked.&lt;/strong&gt; Solution: Update dependencies or pin versions compatible with &lt;code&gt;setuptools&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Conclusion: The Optimal Migration Path
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The deprecation isn’t just a deadline—it’s a technical cliff. The optimal solution is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Migrate directly to Python 3.12+.&lt;/strong&gt; It avoids the temporary fix of 3.11 and aligns with AWS’s long-term support.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Address &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt;, removed modules, and OS changes.&lt;/strong&gt; These are the primary failure points.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rebuild compiled wheels/layers for AL2023.&lt;/strong&gt; Binary compatibility is non-negotiable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Failure to migrate by October 2026 results in: &lt;strong&gt;Security exposure → Operational paralysis → Application decay.&lt;/strong&gt; The choice is mechanical: Migrate or break.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Migration Scenarios: Navigating the Python 3.10 to 3.12+ Transition on AWS Lambda
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Migrating Python Lambda functions from 3.10 to 3.12+ isn’t just a version bump—it’s a technical overhaul. Below are six common scenarios developers face, dissected with causal explanations and optimal solutions. Each scenario highlights the mechanical process behind the breakage and the most effective fix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. &lt;strong&gt;distutils Removal in Python 3.12&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Impact:&lt;/em&gt; Legacy &lt;code&gt;setup.py&lt;/code&gt; scripts or libraries importing &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; fail with &lt;code&gt;ModuleNotFoundError&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; Python 3.12 drops &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; entirely, breaking any code that directly or transitively relies on it. The interpreter no longer recognizes the module, halting execution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Solution:&lt;/em&gt; Replace &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;setuptools&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;packaging&lt;/code&gt;. For transitive dependencies (e.g., &lt;code&gt;scikit-learn &amp;lt; 1.3&lt;/code&gt;), update the library or pin to a compatible version. &lt;strong&gt;Rule:&lt;/strong&gt; If &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; is detected in imports or dependencies, refactor to &lt;code&gt;setuptools&lt;/code&gt;—it’s a drop-in replacement in most cases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. &lt;strong&gt;Removed Modules in Python 3.13 (PEP 594)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Impact:&lt;/em&gt; Code using &lt;code&gt;cgi&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;crypt&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;telnetlib&lt;/code&gt; crashes at runtime.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; These modules are flagged for removal in Python 3.13, and their absence triggers &lt;code&gt;NameError&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;ImportError&lt;/code&gt; exceptions. The interpreter cannot resolve the module names.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Solution:&lt;/em&gt; Refactor code to use modern alternatives. For example, replace &lt;code&gt;crypt&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;hashlib&lt;/code&gt; for hashing. &lt;strong&gt;Rule:&lt;/strong&gt; If legacy modules are in use, audit and replace them before migrating to 3.13. Ignoring this breaks runtime execution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. &lt;strong&gt;OS Shift to Amazon Linux 2023&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Impact:&lt;/em&gt; Compiled wheels or layers (e.g., &lt;code&gt;numpy&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;psycopg2&lt;/code&gt;) fail with &lt;code&gt;ImportError&lt;/code&gt; or segmentation faults.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; Python 3.12+ runs on Amazon Linux 2023, which uses a newer glibc version. Wheels compiled for Amazon Linux 2 (AL2) are incompatible due to binary differences in system libraries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Solution:&lt;/em&gt; Rebuild wheels and layers specifically for Amazon Linux 2023. Use tools like &lt;code&gt;docker&lt;/code&gt; to emulate the AL2023 environment for local compilation. &lt;strong&gt;Rule:&lt;/strong&gt; If using compiled extensions, rebuild them for AL2023—AL2 binaries will not work. Skipping this step causes runtime failures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  4. &lt;strong&gt;Python 3.11 as a Temporary Stopgap&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Impact:&lt;/em&gt; Migrating to Python 3.11 delays the inevitable, as it’s deprecated on June 30, 2027.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; Python 3.11 remains on AL2, avoiding the OS shift issue, but its short support window makes it a suboptimal choice. It’s a temporary fix, not a long-term solution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Solution:&lt;/em&gt; Migrate directly to Python 3.12+ to align with AWS’s long-term support (LTS) strategy. &lt;strong&gt;Rule:&lt;/strong&gt; If time permits, skip 3.11—it’s a detour. Direct migration to 3.12+ avoids double effort and ensures compliance with AWS’s timeline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  5. &lt;strong&gt;Hidden Transitive Dependencies&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Impact:&lt;/em&gt; Seemingly unrelated libraries fail due to transitive &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; dependencies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; Libraries like &lt;code&gt;scikit-learn &amp;lt; 1.3&lt;/code&gt; rely on &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; internally. When &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; is removed in 3.12, these libraries break, even if your code doesn’t directly import it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Solution:&lt;/em&gt; Update or pin dependencies to versions compatible with &lt;code&gt;setuptools&lt;/code&gt;. Use tools like &lt;code&gt;pipdeptree&lt;/code&gt; to identify hidden dependencies. &lt;strong&gt;Rule:&lt;/strong&gt; If a library fails post-migration, check its dependencies for &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; usage. Update or pin to fix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  6. &lt;strong&gt;Console Edit Block Post-Deprecation&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Impact:&lt;/em&gt; Unable to edit Python 3.10 functions via the AWS console after October 31, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; AWS disables console edits by rejecting API requests (400 Bad Request) for Python 3.10 functions. The backend API blocks modifications to deprecated runtimes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Solution:&lt;/em&gt; Migrate to 3.12+ before the deadline to retain console edit functionality. For urgent hotfixes post-deprecation, use CLI or SDKs to update code. &lt;strong&gt;Rule:&lt;/strong&gt; If console edits are critical, migrate before October 2026. Post-deprecation, rely on CLI/SDK for updates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Optimal Migration Path
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Direct Migration to 3.12+:&lt;/strong&gt; Avoids temporary fixes and aligns with AWS LTS.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Address Key Breakages:&lt;/strong&gt; Prioritize &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt;, removed modules, and OS changes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rebuild Compiled Artifacts:&lt;/strong&gt; Ensure wheels/layers are AL2023-compatible.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Professional Judgment:&lt;/strong&gt; Migrating to Python 3.12+ is non-negotiable for security and operational continuity. Delaying risks unpatched vulnerabilities, console lockouts, and deployment halts. The optimal strategy is proactive migration, addressing technical debt before the October 2026 deadline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Best Practices &amp;amp; Tools for Migrating Python 3.10 Lambda Functions to 3.12+
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS’s deprecation of Python 3.10 for Lambda functions isn’t just a nudge—it’s a hard deadline with tangible consequences. By October 31, 2026, failing to migrate means losing security patches, console edit capabilities, and eventually, the ability to create or update functions. The transition to Python 3.12+ isn’t trivial, but with the right strategies and tools, it’s manageable. Here’s how to navigate the migration without breaking your production systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Identify Affected Functions: The First Domino
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before you can fix what’s broken, you need to know what’s broken. AWS doesn’t flag deprecated runtimes proactively, so you’re on your own. Use the following command to identify Python 3.10 Lambda functions per region:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Command:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws lambda list-functions --function-version ALL --query "Functions[?Runtime=='python3.10'].FunctionName,Version" --output text&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; This CLI query filters all Lambda functions by runtime, returning the function name and version. It’s a direct API call to AWS’s Lambda service, bypassing the console’s lack of deprecation warnings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Tackle the Python 3.12+ Breaking Changes: Where Things Actually Fail
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  a. &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; Removal: The Silent Killer
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Impact:&lt;/strong&gt; Python 3.12 drops &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt;, causing &lt;code&gt;ModuleNotFoundError&lt;/code&gt; in legacy &lt;code&gt;setup.py&lt;/code&gt; scripts or libraries that import it directly or transitively.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; The Python interpreter no longer recognizes &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt;, breaking build processes that rely on it. Transitive dependencies (e.g., &lt;code&gt;scikit-learn &amp;lt; 1.3&lt;/code&gt;) may fail silently, blocking deployments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Solution:&lt;/strong&gt; Replace &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;setuptools&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;packaging&lt;/code&gt;. Use &lt;code&gt;pipdeptree&lt;/code&gt; to identify hidden dependencies and update/pin them to compatible versions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Rule:&lt;/em&gt; If your build pipeline uses &lt;code&gt;setup.py&lt;/code&gt; or libraries pre-2022, assume &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; is a risk. Migrate to &lt;code&gt;setuptools&lt;/code&gt; and audit dependencies with &lt;code&gt;pipdeptree&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  b. Removed Modules (&lt;code&gt;cgi&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;crypt&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;telnetlib&lt;/code&gt;): The Dead Batteries
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Impact:&lt;/strong&gt; Python 3.13 removes these modules, causing &lt;code&gt;NameError&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;ImportError&lt;/code&gt; at runtime.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; PEP 594 flags these modules for removal, and Python 3.13 enforces it. Legacy code using these modules will crash immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Solution:&lt;/strong&gt; Replace &lt;code&gt;crypt&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;hashlib&lt;/code&gt;, refactor &lt;code&gt;cgi&lt;/code&gt; usage with modern web frameworks, and deprecate &lt;code&gt;telnetlib&lt;/code&gt; entirely. Test replacements in isolation before deploying.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Rule:&lt;/em&gt; If your codebase predates 2010, assume these modules are present. Use static analysis tools like &lt;code&gt;pylint&lt;/code&gt; to flag imports and refactor proactively.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  c. OS Shift to Amazon Linux 2023: The Binary Incompatibility
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Impact:&lt;/strong&gt; Compiled wheels and layers (e.g., &lt;code&gt;numpy&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;psycopg2&lt;/code&gt;) built for Amazon Linux 2 fail on AL2023 with &lt;code&gt;ImportError&lt;/code&gt; or segmentation faults.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; AL2023 uses a newer glibc version, making binaries compiled for AL2 incompatible. The Python interpreter attempts to load these binaries but fails due to missing symbols or ABI mismatches.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Solution:&lt;/strong&gt; Rebuild wheels and layers for AL2023 using Docker containers. Example Dockerfile:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight docker"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.12RUN pip install numpy --platform manylinux2014_x86_64 --target /python&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Rule:&lt;/em&gt; If your Lambda uses compiled extensions, rebuild them for AL2023. Skip this step only if your dependencies are pure Python.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Testing Strategies: Catch Failures Before Production
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Migration without testing is a gamble. Here’s how to de-risk it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unit Tests:&lt;/strong&gt; Mock AWS Lambda context and test function logic in isolation. Focus on code paths using removed modules or compiled extensions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Integration Tests:&lt;/strong&gt; Deploy migrated functions to a staging environment with AL2023. Invoke them with sample events and verify responses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Canary Deployments:&lt;/strong&gt; Route 10% of production traffic to the migrated function. Monitor for errors using CloudWatch Alarms.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; Testing catches failures at different layers—unit tests for code logic, integration tests for environment compatibility, and canary deployments for real-world behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Automation Tools: Scale the Migration
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Manual migration is error-prone and slow. Automate where possible:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;AWS SAM CLI:&lt;/strong&gt; Use SAM templates to define Lambda functions and dependencies. Automate builds and deployments with &lt;code&gt;sam build&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;sam deploy&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CI/CD Pipelines:&lt;/strong&gt; Integrate migration steps into your CI/CD pipeline. Example GitHub Actions workflow:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;jobs: migrate: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v3 - run: pip install -r requirements-3.12.txt - run: sam build --use-container - run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;sam deploy --resolve-s3&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; Automation ensures consistency and repeatability. CI/CD pipelines enforce migration steps, reducing human error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  5. Edge Cases: What Could Still Break
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even with careful migration, edge cases lurk:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Transitive Dependencies:&lt;/strong&gt; Libraries like &lt;code&gt;scikit-learn &amp;lt; 1.3&lt;/code&gt; rely on &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt;. Update or pin these dependencies to avoid silent failures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Custom Layers:&lt;/strong&gt; Layers compiled for AL2 won’t work on AL2023. Rebuild them using Docker and the AL2023 base image.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Python 3.11 as a Stopgap:&lt;/strong&gt; While Python 3.11 avoids the OS shift, it’s deprecated by June 30, 2027. Migrate directly to 3.12+ to avoid double migration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Rule:&lt;/em&gt; If you encounter an edge case, document it and share it with your team. Edge cases are rare but catastrophic if overlooked.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Professional Judgment: Migrate Directly to 3.12+ Before October 2026
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Python 3.11 is a temporary fix with a shorter lifespan. Migrating directly to 3.12+ aligns with AWS’s long-term support strategy, avoids the OS shift twice, and future-proofs your Lambda functions. Address &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt;, removed modules, and OS changes as the primary failure points. Rebuild compiled wheels/layers for AL2023 to ensure binary compatibility. Failing to migrate proactively risks security exposure, operational paralysis, and application decay.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mechanism:&lt;/em&gt; Direct migration to 3.12+ minimizes technical debt and maximizes compatibility with AWS’s roadmap. Temporary fixes introduce additional migration cycles, increasing risk and cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusion &amp;amp; Resources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS’s deprecation of Python 3.10 for Lambda is a critical shift that demands immediate action. The &lt;strong&gt;October 31, 2026&lt;/strong&gt; deadline isn’t just a date—it’s the point where your functions stop receiving security patches, become uneditable via the console, and eventually face creation and update blocks. This isn’t a gradual fade-out; it’s a hard stop that risks exposing your applications to unpatched vulnerabilities and operational paralysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Migrating to Python 3.12+ isn’t just about compliance—it’s about &lt;em&gt;future-proofing your codebase&lt;/em&gt;. The technical challenges are real: &lt;strong&gt;distutils is gone&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Amazon Linux 2023 replaces AL2&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;hidden dependencies&lt;/strong&gt; like transitive &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; imports can break your builds. But the solutions are clear:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Replace &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;setuptools&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;packaging&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. This isn’t optional—legacy &lt;code&gt;setup.py&lt;/code&gt; scripts will fail without it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rebuild compiled wheels and layers for AL2023&lt;/strong&gt;. If you skip this, extensions like &lt;code&gt;numpy&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;psycopg2&lt;/code&gt; will crash at runtime due to &lt;em&gt;glibc incompatibility&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Audit dependencies with &lt;code&gt;pipdeptree&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. Libraries like &lt;code&gt;scikit-learn &amp;lt; 1.3&lt;/code&gt; silently rely on &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt;, and updating them is non-negotiable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Python 3.11 is a &lt;em&gt;trap&lt;/em&gt;. It’s deprecated by June 30, 2027, and still runs on AL2, meaning you’ll face the same OS shift twice. &lt;strong&gt;Migrate directly to 3.12+&lt;/strong&gt;—it’s the only path that aligns with AWS’s long-term support strategy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To identify affected functions, use this command per Region:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;aws lambda list-functions --function-version ALL --query "Functions[?Runtime=='python3.10'].FunctionName,Version" --output text&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For deeper insights and official resources, refer to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://eolwatch.pages.dev/guides/lambda-python-3-10-deprecation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Lambda Python 3.10 Deprecation Guide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/migrating-your-aws-lambda-functions-from-python-3-10-to-python-3-12/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Official Migration Blog&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-runtimes.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Lambda Runtime Support Policy&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Professional Judgment:&lt;/strong&gt; Delaying migration is a gamble with security and operational stability. If you’re running Python 3.10, start migrating to 3.12+ now. Address &lt;code&gt;distutils&lt;/code&gt;, OS changes, and dependencies as your top priorities. Rebuild compiled artifacts for AL2023, and automate testing to catch edge cases early. The deadline is firm, and the risks of ignoring it are catastrophic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Watching people demo their projects was the moment hosting Hacktoberfest really hit me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They had arrived with ideas. Now they were showing what they had built, explaining their thinking, and getting excited about their own work. I was excited right along with them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fcps2unmf3dsvfnwr3cha.jpeg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fcps2unmf3dsvfnwr3cha.jpeg" alt="Demo" width="768" height="1024"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I remember thinking: &lt;em&gt;I helped create the space for this to happen.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That feeling brought me back to an article I published in 2017 about attending my first hackathon. Back then, I was writing about how much the experience had given me. This time, I was getting to see it from the other side.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But the decision to host started much more simply: I was scrolling through Dev.to.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I came across a call for Hacktoberfest hosts. I already knew Hacktoberfest and tried to participate each year. Traditionally, it centered on contributing to open source through pull requests. This year’s format included in-person events focused on learning and building with open-source AI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I looked around for an event in my area and couldn’t find one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As co-lead of AI Collective’s Maryland chapter, I thought this would be a good opportunity for our community. We could get people together, give them time to work on ideas, and see what they could build.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I raised my hand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Saying yes was the easy part
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hosting meant finding a venue, coordinating with people, arranging the space, and getting the word out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I knew there would be work involved. Doing it gave me a much better understanding of how many things have to come together before an attendee can simply walk in, open a laptop, and get started.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Promotion took effort, too. Around half the people who registered actually attended a useful lesson in the difference between sign-ups and turnout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My expectation going in was pretty simple: if one person came and had a good experience, I would consider that a win.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then the demos happened.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;People were happy to show what they had made. They had used the day to explore their ideas, collaborate, and build something they could share. Watching that unfold was more rewarding than I had expected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Until then, I had been doing the work of organizing an event. During the demos, I got to enjoy what that work had made possible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The tools have changed since my first hackathon
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At my first hackathon, organized by Women Who Code in DC, I joined a team working on an emergency management idea.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We had plenty of ideas and limited time. By the end, we had created a website mockup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I still left inspired. I had met people with different experiences, learned how they approached problems, and had the chance to contribute what I knew.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At our Hacktoberfest event, Gemini and other AI tools helped people take their ideas further within a single day. Seeing what they could build and demo in that time was exciting. We didn’t have those tools at my first hackathon.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also made me appreciate having a dedicated place and time to use them together. People could bring an idea, work alongside others, and have something to show by the end of the day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I recognized their excitement, even though the tools and projects were different from my first experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Then I went back and read my old article
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href="https://medium.com/@mercygreen/hackathon-what-i-learned-and-why-you-should-attend-5fdccb6b9c39" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;that 2017 post&lt;/a&gt;, I wrote:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“When I started sharing what I know with other people, I realized that what I have been doing has a value”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At that first hackathon, sharing what I knew helped me recognize what I could contribute. Hosting expanded my understanding of what contributing can look like. Finding a space, bringing people together, and giving them time to work on their ideas made room for everyone else’s contributions, too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you’ve enjoyed attending events like these, consider helping make one happen. You could help organize through a local chapter, find a venue, or bring a few people together for a day of building. There’s work involved, but you don’t have to start with a big event.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first hackathon left me excited about what I could do. Hosting Hacktoberfest left me excited about what I could help other people do.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What’s something an organizer did that made a developer event memorable for you? I’d love to hear what made it worth showing up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tal vez esta historia te suene conocida: un componente empieza a fallar, hay poca información y mucha presión. Hay que actuar rápido, cada minuto cuenta, sientes el peso en los hombros y terminas con dolor de espalda. Cada incidente requiere un análisis detallado, preciso y de calidad. Y ojalá que para ayer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;¿Cómo mitigo esa presión? Rodeándome de herramientas que me permitan ubicarme rápidamente. En mi caso, Azure Monitor, con sus métricas para ir descartando sospechosos y acorralar al culpable, y después las consultas KQL para profundizar en la investigación.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Te cuento una anécdota, muy breve.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Se me reportó un problema de rendimiento que implicó revisar métricas, formular hipótesis y ejecutar varias consultas KQL. Después de un rato tenía un informe que, modestia aparte, había quedado bellísimo. Con la evidencia&amp;nbsp;pude entender mejor el problema y tomar acción. No podía sentirme más orgullosa.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lo curioso vino después. Al tiempo, me encontré con una opción nueva en Azure Monitor: &lt;strong&gt;Observability Agent &lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;La curiosidad ganó. ¿Podría ayudarme a investigar un problema como el que había analizado?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Le describí el síntoma en lenguaje natural, le indiqué la ventana de tiempo y los servicios involucrados. ¿Y qué crees? En cuestión de minutos generó la mayoría de las consultas que yo había escrito, y algunas más. Además, preparó un informe bien redactado y, como si no me hubiera impresionado lo suficiente, agregó cuatro hermosos gráficos que ayudaban a visualizar el problema.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;¿Significaba que podía olvidarme de investigar? No exactamente. Pero sí que podía dedicar menos tiempo a construir consultas desde cero y más tiempo a interpretar las evidencias.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En este artículo te cuento un poquito sobre Azure Copilot Observability Agent, y qué debes tener en cuenta para aprovecharlo. Aviso: esto es solo un vistazo desde 100.000 pies de altura. El agente tiene más capacidades que las que exploré en esta experiencia.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;¿Qué es Azure Copilot Observability Agent?&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El &lt;strong&gt;Azure Copilot Observability Agent&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;es un asistente con inteligencia artificial integrado en Azure Monitor. No reemplaza lo que ya conoces: métricas, logs, Application Insights, alertas y dashboards. Su propósito es ayudarte a trabajar con las señales de observabilidad mediante lenguaje natural, investigaciones guiadas y correlación de información.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En lugar de saltar de una pantalla a otra para armar el rompecabezas a mano, puedes pedirle que explore los datos, genere consultas y te ayude a entender qué está pasando.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Y un detalle importante: &lt;strong&gt;no es una herramienta estática&lt;/strong&gt;. Sus capacidades están evolucionando y algunas funciones todavía se encuentran en versión preliminar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;¿Qué puedes hacer con él?&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Chatear con tus datos.&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Puedes explorar métricas, logs y otras señales de telemetría utilizando lenguaje natural. También puedes pedirle que genere consultas para profundizar en un comportamiento.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Investigar un problema.&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Las investigaciones profundas permiten analizar señales de distintos componentes, buscar correlaciones y generar un informe con hallazgos y posibles siguientes pasos.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Explorar operaciones autónomas . El agente puede correlacionar alertas en segundo plano, crear &lt;em&gt;issues&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;en Azure Monitor e iniciar investigaciones profundas sobre ellos. Requieren crear un recurso de Observability Agent y solo están disponibles en ciertas regiones. En mi práctica, las decisiones de mitigación y los cambios en el entorno los sigo tomando yo. Consulta la &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/aiops/observability-agent-autonomous-operations" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentación oficial sobre operaciones autónomas&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;para conocer las capacidades, los requisitos y las regiones admitidas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;¿Quién puede usarlo?&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Necesitas acceso a Azure Copilot. Por eso, dentro de una misma organización, es posible que algunos usuarios puedan utilizarlo y otros no.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Un detalle sobre el idioma&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En mis pruebas pude interactuar con el agente tanto en español como en inglés. Sin embargo, la documentación indica que el inglés es el idioma admitido y que otros idiomas tienen soporte limitado. En mi experiencia, los informes se generaron en inglés.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Como siempre, conviene distinguir entre lo que funcionó en una prueba y lo que está oficialmente soportado.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Al investigar, úsalo con criterio&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El secreto de cualquier agente de IA es darle buen contexto. Cuanto mejor sea tu especificación, más posibilidades tendrás de obtener resultados útiles.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No es lo mismo decirle «averigua por qué falla mi aplicación» que dirigir la investigación hacia donde realmente tiene sentido.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Primero, ten buenos dashboards&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No hay recetas que funcionen para todas las organizaciones, pero te comparto un tip:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Un buen dashboard no es un grupo de gráficos bonitos. Un buen dashboard te cuenta una historia.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Debe ayudarte a entender cómo se comporta tu aplicación, qué tan disponible está y dónde podrían estar los problemas de rendimiento.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En una aplicación con varios servicios, por ejemplo, debería ayudarte a responder preguntas como estas:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Cuánto tarda mi aplicación en responder al cliente?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Está funcionando todo como debería?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Hay latencia en alguna dependencia?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Cómo se comporta el servicio de búsqueda?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Qué componente muestra señales que merecen una investigación más profunda?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un dashboard no necesariamente te dará la causa raíz, pero sí puede ayudarte a decidir por dónde empezar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Aférrate a los hechos&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Es probable que, por historia, tengas un componente o servicio que consideras el patito feo: ese que siempre da problemas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A mí me ha pasado. Tengo mi favorito, el que siempre tengo entre ojos. Pero eso es una corazonada. A veces acierto, pero muchas veces el sospechoso no era el culpable y mi primer vistazo estaba equivocado.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Los datos nos ayudan a separar las sospechas de las evidencias. Con buenos dashboards puedo formarme una primera idea del problema y, como se dice en mi país,&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;«así sabemos por dónde anda la procesión»&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Esa primera idea es útil, pero no debería convertirse en una conclusión antes de tiempo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;El agente necesita instrucciones claras&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Con los datos de tus dashboards y las señales disponibles en Azure Monitor, ya tienes una base para orientar la investigación.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lo interesante es que, al revisar los resultados, puedes encontrar relaciones que no habías considerado inicialmente. El agente puede ayudarte a explorar esas posibilidades, pero sus conclusiones deben contrastarse con las evidencias.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Como con cualquier herramienta de IA, te recomiendo:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Describe el síntoma.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;¿Qué está ocurriendo? ¿Errores intermitentes, latencia elevada, timeouts o una degradación general del rendimiento?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Indica qué has revisado.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Menciona las validaciones realizadas y los problemas que ya descartaste. Así ayudas a acotar la investigación.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Señala las hipótesis que quieres evaluar.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Puedes dirigirlo hacia determinados componentes y pedirle que busque correlaciones, sin asumir que el sospechoso es necesariamente el culpable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Acota la ventana de tiempo e indica la zona horaria.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Este detalle es importante. En una investigación puedes tener reportes expresados en hora local y telemetría registrada en UTC. Si no alineas las referencias temporales, puedes terminar comparando eventos que no corresponden al mismo momento.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Veamos un ejemplo con un escenario que seguramente les resultará familiar a muchos equipos de soporte.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un usuario está recibiendo &lt;em&gt;timeouts&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;de forma intermitente en la función &lt;strong&gt;F&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ya validé que los recursos &lt;strong&gt;A&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;y &lt;strong&gt;B&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;están disponibles y que no presentan señales de saturación ni acumulación de solicitudes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El componente &lt;strong&gt;C&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;está mostrando latencia. Analiza su comportamiento y correlaciónalo con el rendimiento de la función &lt;strong&gt;F&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;durante la ventana de tiempo &lt;strong&gt;[hora de inicio]&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;a &lt;strong&gt;[hora final]&lt;/strong&gt;, en &lt;strong&gt;UTC-6&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Identifica las evidencias que respaldan o descartan al componente &lt;strong&gt;C&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;como posible origen del problema. Explica las correlaciones encontradas, las hipótesis alternativas y las consultas utilizadas para llegar a tus conclusiones.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;La diferencia está en que no le estoy pidiendo que adivine qué falla. Le estoy dando un síntoma, un contexto y una hipótesis que debe contrastar con los datos.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;El veredicto&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En mi experiencia, el agente encontró correlaciones que respaldaban mi hipótesis y me presentó evidencias que podía contrastar con mi análisis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En cuestión de minutos tenía consultas KQL, un informe y gráficos que me ayudaban a visualizar los hallazgos.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;¿Eso significa que el agente siempre encontrará la causa raíz? No. La calidad de la telemetría, el contexto disponible y el alcance de la investigación influyen en los resultados. Una correlación tampoco demuestra, por sí sola, que un componente sea la causa del problema.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Las consultas y los gráficos requieren revisión. Las conclusiones deben contrastarse con los datos y con el conocimiento que tenemos del sistema.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Y si hay que definir un workaround, esa decisión me corresponde a mí, no al agente.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Conclusión&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. El agente acelera, pero tú apuntas.&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Sin el contexto y las pistas que le proporcioné, la investigación no habría sido la misma.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Mira primero tus dashboards.&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Te ayudan a reconocer patrones y a decidir dónde conviene profundizar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. No tengas favoritismos entre servicios.&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;El que menos sospechas puede ser el que merezca una segunda mirada.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Acota la búsqueda.&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;No es lo mismo explorar todos los servicios involucrados que centrarte en un conjunto de hipótesis. Tampoco es lo mismo analizar un año de telemetría que una ventana de tiempo relacionada con el síntoma.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. Valida lo que te entrega.&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Que una hipótesis parezca confirmada no significa que la causa raíz esté demostrada. Revisa las consultas, los gráficos y las evidencias antes de tomar decisiones.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;6. El costo del servicio. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;El agente usa facturación por consumo, esto&amp;nbsp;entró en vigor el 1 de julio de 2026, y una investigación profunda consume más trabajo y por tanto suele costar más, así que asegúrate de mantener acotado el alcance de las investigaciones.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Esto es solo la punta del iceberg&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lo que te conté corresponde a mi experiencia explorando los datos y solicitando investigaciones puntuales. No he probado todas las capacidades del agente, y todavía queda mucho por explorar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;La observabilidad sigue siendo una disciplina que requiere buenas métricas, telemetría útil, contexto y criterio técnico. La IA puede ayudarnos a recorrer ese camino más rápido, pero no elimina la necesidad de comprender los sistemas que operamos.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Y esa es, para mí, la parte más interesante: no se trata de dejar de investigar, sino de cambiar la forma en que invertimos nuestro tiempo al hacerlo.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  &amp;nbsp;Referencias 
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/aiops/observability-agent-overview" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Azure Copilot Observability Agent (Microsoft Learn)&lt;/a&gt; &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/aiops/observability-agent-deep-investigations" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Deep investigations in the Azure Copilot Observability Agent (Microsoft Learn)&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/aiops/observability-agent-autonomous-operations" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Autonomous operations in the Azure Copilot Observability Agent (Microsoft Learn)&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/aiops/observability-agent-resource" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Observability Agent resource, preview (Microsoft Learn)&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/azureobservabilityblog/-/4469041" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Advancing Full-Stack Observability with Azure Monitor at Ignite 2025 (Azure Observability Blog)&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; &lt;a href="https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/azure-observability-blog/understanding-billing-for-the-azure-copilot-observability-agent/ba-p/4537780" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Understanding billing for the Azure Copilot Observability Agent (Azure Observability Blog)&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The deploy job turns green at 2:14 p.m. Someone drops the link in the team channel, and the on-call engineer opens the dashboards. Error rates and latency are holding flat. Ten minutes later, the team calls the change good. But when those dashboards opened, a third of the pods were still on the previous version. One region didn't finish syncing until 2:31. The flat graphs mostly showed the old code behaving the way it always had.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pipeline reported accurately on everything it controls, and nothing in it failed. The mistake was in how the team read the green check. The check confirmed that the pipeline had finished its steps, but the new code hadn't reached most of the traffic yet, so the team judged a change that had barely run. Every signal the team looked at described the deploy, and none came from the change itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A change passes through four events
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The green check marks just one of four events a change passes through on its way to users. Many teams already keep two of them apart, deploying code to production behind a feature flag and releasing the new behavior to users later. Even so, they tend to treat each event as proof of the next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CI finishes first. Then the code deploys. Next, the code is running everywhere, in every environment and region that users actually reach. Finally, the feature releases, and users can experience the new behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those events don't always line up. The pipeline can report success while the new code is still rolling out to some pods or regions. That gap between deploying the code and running it everywhere usually closes within minutes, so most checks skip it. There are three scenarios where this lag is most common:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Kubernetes rollout or GitOps sync can report success while pods are still restarting or failing readiness checks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A CDN can keep serving stale assets from edge caches until their TTL runs out or someone purges them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A multi-region deploy can finish in one region well before another.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In each case, the deploy finishes before the change has handled real traffic, so verification has to confirm that the change ran before its metrics can mean anything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Existing signals describe the deploy
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The signals most often used to confirm a change is live describe the deploy as a whole.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Signal&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Proves&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Doesn't prove&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rollout status or sync result&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The pipeline finished its steps&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Every instance is serving new code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Health and readiness checks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The process is up and accepting traffic&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The new code path is running&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;/version&lt;/code&gt; endpoint&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Which build an instance has&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;That users reach that instance or that code path&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Deploy markers in observability&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;When the deploy happened&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;When the change actually took effect&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each of these signals answers a valid question about the deploy. A status field reports what the pipeline believes happened. A fixed bake time proves only that time passed. A quiet dashboard is an inference drawn from silence. Canary analysis gets closer, but it still compares aggregate metrics across versions, and aggregates can't show that any one change in a build ever ran. None of these signals shows that a specific change ran in production for an actual request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The change leaves its own evidence
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most obvious path is often the most overlooked, and feature flags are a good example. A flag inside the new code can be the signal that proves it's running. Say a flag key exists only in the new build. An evaluation of that flag can happen only if the new build is running and a request has reached that code path. The evaluation is therefore a signal the code produces about itself. For instance, in a product catalog, this LaunchDarkly flag check sits in the new code path that ranks search results.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ldclient&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ldclient&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ldclient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;search_products&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;builder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;build&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# This flag only exists in the new build. If it's being evaluated,
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# at least one instance is running the new code.
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;variation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;new-catalog-ranker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="bp"&gt;False&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;new_ranking&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;legacy_ranking&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A team already wrapping risky changes in a release flag can use that same flag to carry this evidence without extra instrumentation. The flag can stay off the whole time, and users keep getting the legacy ranking. Each call still counts as an evaluation as long as the SDK sends events. That team can therefore see that the new code is running before it releases anything to users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fa63wpfg116stb9qm6yg7.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fa63wpfg116stb9qm6yg7.png" alt="LaunchDarkly Evaluations chart for new-catalog-ranker in production over the past hour, with all evaluations on the Legacy ranker variation" width="799" height="467"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LaunchDarkly shows those evaluations per environment, over time, and by variation. After a deploy, an engineer can check whether the new flag has been evaluated in production at all. An empty chart means either no instance is running the new code or no request has reached it yet. SDKs send events in batches, so a minute or two of silence right after a deploy is normal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rollout status still tracks whether every pod and region has the new build. Evaluations answer the question rollout status can't, which is whether the change has handled requests yet. Once the change has handled requests, its metrics can start to say something about it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Any signal emitted from inside the new code path proves the same thing, whether it's a metric, a log line, or a trace. A rarely hit code path may take a while to show any evidence at all. If no request has reached the new code, nothing in production has tested it yet. That check is easy to run by hand on one change and one code path, and harder when a single deploy carries dozens of agent-written changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Agent volume widens the gap
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Coding agents raise the number of changes in each deploy, so evidence per change matters more. When a deploy carries a few human-written changes, the gap stays small and easy to catch. Someone usually notices that the dashboards look too calm and checks again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At agent speed, "the deploy is live" says little about any one change. It can't show which changes are running or which code paths have received requests. It also can't show which changes are still waiting on a cache or a region.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cost of checking too early repeats with every change. A change judged against old code was never really tested, yet the team still decides to keep it, ramp it up, or move on. At a handful of changes a week, that cost is a rounding error. At agent volume, the same mistake can hit many of the changes in a single deploy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the unit of verification moves from the deploy to the change. Each change needs its own evidence that it's running before anyone decides what happens to it next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Know what you're waiting for
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before a release starts, the team should already know which evidence it's waiting for, such as a first evaluation in production, a log line from the new path, or a version-tagged trace. A finished pipeline gives the team permission to start checking. Evidence from production decides the verdict.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Collecting that evidence is the first step. The decisions that follow depend on it, such as whether to ramp up the rollout, pause, or roll back. As agents add more changes to every deploy, teams will make those decisions more often. They'll also have less time to spend on each change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Los agentes de código son increíbles… hasta que ejecutan &lt;code&gt;git reset --hard&lt;/code&gt; sobre una tarde de trabajo sin commitear.&lt;br&gt;
Claude Code tiene una función poco conocida para evitarlo: &lt;strong&gt;los hooks&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Qué es un hook
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un script que Claude Code ejecuta &lt;strong&gt;antes o después&lt;/strong&gt; de usar una herramienta. Si un hook &lt;code&gt;PreToolUse&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;
termina con &lt;strong&gt;código de salida 2&lt;/strong&gt;, la acción se cancela y Claude recibe tu mensaje de error, así que corrige su plan solo.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Hook 1: bloquear comandos destructivos
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;.claude/hooks/bloquear.py&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;#!/usr/bin/env python3
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;re&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;PATRONES&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;\brm\s+-[a-zA-Z]*[rR][a-zA-Z]*f?\s+(/|~|\$HOME|\*)(\s|$)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;rm recursivo sobre /, ~ o *&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;\bgit\s+push\b.*(--force\b|-f\b)(?!-with-lease)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;git push --force&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;\bgit\s+reset\s+--hard\b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;git reset --hard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;(curl|wget)[^|]*\|\s*(sudo\s+)?(ba|z)?sh\b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;curl | sh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;cmd&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;load&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;stdin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;tool_input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;or&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{}).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;command&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;motivo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PATRONES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;re&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;search&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cmd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;re&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;I&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Bloqueado: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;motivo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;stderr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;exit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Hook 2: que no lea tu &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mismo patrón, pero mirando &lt;code&gt;tool_input.file_path&lt;/code&gt; en las herramientas &lt;code&gt;Read|Edit|Write&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;
y bloqueando &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;*.pem&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;id_rsa&lt;/code&gt;… (y permitiendo &lt;code&gt;.env.example&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Conectarlos
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;.claude/settings.json&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"hooks"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"PreToolUse"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
      &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"matcher"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Bash"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
        &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"hooks"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"command"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"command"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"python3 &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/.claude/hooks/bloquear.py"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Reinicia Claude Code y escribe &lt;code&gt;/hooks&lt;/code&gt; para comprobar que está activo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Probarlo sin riesgo
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{"tool_input":{"command":"rm -rf ~"}}'&lt;/span&gt; | python3 .claude/hooks/bloquear.py&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$?&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c"&gt;# 2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{"tool_input":{"command":"npm test"}}'&lt;/span&gt;  | python3 .claude/hooks/bloquear.py&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$?&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c"&gt;# 0&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Código completo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Los dos hooks probados y una skill de code review en español, gratis: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/carlosp5751258-lgtm/claude-code-es" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/carlosp5751258-lgtm/claude-code-es&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
Si quieres la configuración completa (6 skills, comandos &lt;code&gt;/plan&lt;/code&gt; y &lt;code&gt;/pr&lt;/code&gt;, plantillas CLAUDE.md, autoformato),&lt;br&gt;
está el &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://claudecodees.gumroad.com/l/kit-claude-code-es" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Kit Claude Code en Español&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I asked our AI YouTube Video Maker for a film about the size of the universe. It drew the universe. It also drew ship hulls, loose rocks and random planets that nobody asked for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I build Ziztu, so weigh that. That test is why this week's update to the AI YouTube Video Maker is mostly about restraint: one art style for the whole film, no props the script never mentions, a script you read before anything is drawn, and real photos when a film names a real person. Here is how it works now, step by step, with real screens from &lt;a href="https://getziztu.com/ai-youtube-video-maker?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=lf-update-oct29" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;getziztu.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What it is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You paste a YouTube link or type an idea. You get a narrated film of 3 to 60 minutes for a faceless channel, with the script, the pictures, the voice, thumbnails, titles and a description. The price in credits is shown before the film is made.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Folvogprxzq3ox1m86w5s.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Folvogprxzq3ox1m86w5s.jpg" alt="The AI YouTube Video Maker page: a box that says &amp;quot;The history of the Silk Road in 30 minutes&amp;quot;, with a Look picker, a Length picker and a Make my video button, over a wall of pictures from finished films" width="800" height="313"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The start screen. One box takes a link or an idea. Look and length are chosen right there.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What is new this week
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One-click Generate.&lt;/strong&gt; Link or idea in, a setup sheet, then the whole chain runs to the script. There is one stop, at the finished script, where you read it and then approve the price.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Openers that hook.&lt;/strong&gt; The first lines now follow opener rules: say who, when and where inside two sentences, then pull the viewer in.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A sample for every narrator.&lt;/strong&gt; 33 voices, each with a short description and a sample you can play. Roger is the default for history films, and a voice you pick always wins.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Copy a pasted video's drawing style.&lt;/strong&gt; Paste a link and the film is drawn in that video's style, with our own guide character, not a copy of theirs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Real people shown the right way.&lt;/strong&gt; Real photos with a credit when the look is photographic, drawn likenesses when it is a cartoon, or both.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One art style, no added props.&lt;/strong&gt; Every picture follows the same look, and the pictures show what the script says.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1: Paste a link or type an idea
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two ways in:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Type an idea:&lt;/strong&gt; "Why Taiwan makes the chips", "How Roman roads were built", "The size of the universe".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Paste a YouTube link&lt;/strong&gt; to a video that is working. The tool studies how it is built: the title pattern, the hook, the chapters and the pace. A link only works when the video's captions can be read.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Either way, you can also keep a video's structure and change the subject. That is the useful one for a channel: you take a format that works and tell your own story in it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 2: Press Generate and read the script
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generate opens one setup sheet: the look, the length (any whole number of minutes from 3 to 60), the tone, the narrator, and Basic or Pro with the estimate. Then it plans and writes, chapter by chapter, and stops at the script.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That stop is the point. You read the whole script before a single picture is drawn. Change any line. The tool shows an &lt;strong&gt;originality score&lt;/strong&gt; for how far your words are from the source video, and marks passages that sit too close. Facts the writer is not sure of are flagged, and you can ask for a web check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No picture is bought before you press "Make the film".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3: Pick a look and keep it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are 18 looks. One look carries every picture of the film, so a 40-minute video does not drift from one style to another halfway through.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F79yqdvmkqc2k2rmx0d2x.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F79yqdvmkqc2k2rmx0d2x.jpg" alt="A grid of the looks: Map and cast, Old masters, Engraving, Cinematic real, Anime documentary, Paper cutout, Bedtime history, Cartoon comedy, Geometric Explainer, 3D Reconstruction, Archive Collage, Clay Stop-Motion, Brick World, Miniature World, Deep Space, Blueprint Cutaway, Dark Tech and Manga Ink" width="800" height="407"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The looks, with what each is good for: Map and cast for the big history explainers, Old masters for empires and wars, Engraving for mysteries, Deep Space for planets and galaxies.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Leave it on "Picked for you" and the tool chooses a look that fits your topic. The realistic look needs YouTube's label for altered or synthetic content, and the tool reminds you when it is time to upload.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 4: Check that every shot fits the words
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every shot gets a picture drawn for the line the narrator says over it. Here are six pictures from a short film about the size of the universe, each next to its line:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F56aaabf9g53nh0y7s09f.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F56aaabf9g53nh0y7s09f.jpg" alt="Six pictures of Earth, the Sun, a supercluster, the observable universe, a field of galaxies and one galaxy, each captioned with the narrator's line and a timestamp" width="800" height="426"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;"The Sun is the next stop, about 150 million kilometres away." The picture is the Sun. No ship hulls this time.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A map is drawn only for the part of the story that is about a place. Data cards and diagrams appear when numbers or a process carry the point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 5: Copy a style, with your own guide
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the new part I am proudest of. When you paste a link, the film can be drawn the way that video is drawn: the palette, the line, the level of detail. It takes two style frames from the video and uses them as the look.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What it does not do is copy the channel's mascot. The film gets its own guide, a thin round-head figure with dot eyes in one or two plain garments, so it fits the style without being their character.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8o960g4gnxovdpars1no.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8o960g4gnxovdpars1no.jpg" alt="Five pictures made in the style of a pasted history video: a throne room, a guide next to a framed portrait, a winter river crossing, a second throne room and a map of Russia with relief" width="800" height="94"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Our output from a pasted video of a Russian history channel, drawn in its style with our own guide. Maps use real relief.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I tested this several times before it was good. The first try reinvented the guide, drew too much detail, and put flat maps where the source used satellite relief. That is why the fix is in the update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 6: Real people and real places
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a film names a real person, you choose how they appear:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Drawn in the film's look.&lt;/strong&gt; Made recognisable by public features. The default for stories, comedy and explainers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Their real photo.&lt;/strong&gt; A freely licensed photo from Wikimedia Commons with a small credit line, shown as it is and never redrawn. The default when the look is photographic, so an AI face never stands in for a real person.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Both.&lt;/strong&gt; The real photo when the narrator first says their name, then drawn scenes for the rest of the story. The default for history, politics, news and biography in a drawn look.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F1sbxpwolj1zp3vo5az13.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F1sbxpwolj1zp3vo5az13.jpg" alt="The &amp;quot;Real people and real places, shown the right way&amp;quot; section: Drawn in the film's look, Their real photo and Both" width="800" height="313"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;On the Plan screen you can change the choice for each person. Countries, cities and buildings can appear as real photos with a credit too.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A story about countries can look up who holds the office today, checked on the web, so the film does not name last decade's leader. You can also add a real person yourself, or add up to 3 photos of someone you have the right to use, and they are drawn in your film's look.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 7: Basic or Pro
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both tiers use the same writer, the same narrator voices and the same publish pack. The pictures and the motion are what change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fk8dpna1egrxh10z9wp3q.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fk8dpna1egrxh10z9wp3q.jpg" alt="Two cards: Basic, pictures that move, with FLUX.3 Klein pictures at 1920 by 1088 and no AI video clips; and Pro, real motion, with Nano Banana 2.1 pictures and MiniMax H3 Max Turbo video clips on the key moments" width="800" height="250"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Basic is the lowest price: AI pictures with camera moves, zooms, maps and data cards. Pro adds AI video clips on the key moments.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On both, Claude Sonnet 5.5 writes the script and ElevenLabs reads it. The price in credits is on the button before anything is made.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 8: The publish pack
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the film is done you also get what you need to upload it: title options, three thumbnails, a description with chapter timestamps, tags, a pinned comment and an SRT captions file, with one zip of everything. The topic appears on the thumbnail as a short label.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8fczahtvy1gd0bk6efhp.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8fczahtvy1gd0bk6efhp.jpg" alt="Three thumbnails made for a film about the size of the universe, each with a few bold words and a highlight" width="800" height="149"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Thumbnails for the universe film: "Earth is a...", "Size of the universe, moon to edge" and "93B light-years".&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Download the MP4 and upload it yourself, or send a watch link that anyone with the link can open.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Honest limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A pasted link needs readable captions.&lt;/strong&gt; If the video has none, paste the idea instead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Basic has no AI video clips.&lt;/strong&gt; It is pictures with camera moves. If you want real motion, that is Pro.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;AI writers can be wrong.&lt;/strong&gt; Flagged facts are a hint, not a guarantee. Read the script and use the web check on anything you will state as a fact.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Realistic looks need YouTube's altered-content label.&lt;/strong&gt; The tool reminds you.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A film is not a channel.&lt;/strong&gt; YouTube's rules still apply: it calls repetitive, mass-produced videos "inauthentic content". Using AI is allowed. Publishing videos that could be swapped for each other is the risk.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;400 free credits and no card at &lt;a href="https://getziztu.com/ai-youtube-video-maker?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=lf-update-oct29" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;getziztu.com/ai-youtube-video-maker&lt;/a&gt;. Type one idea, read the script it writes, and only then decide to make the film. If you make one, tell me what it was about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week4-2026-10-26"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 4: Ghost in the Machine&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every security team has ghosts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An audit finding that has been open for weeks. Evidence that nobody can locate. A vendor review waiting for a response. A security exception without a confirmed owner.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They rarely make a dramatic entrance. They sit quietly in spreadsheets, trackers, and follow-up emails until somebody asks the question nobody wants to hear:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Why is this still open?"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Working around the Business Information Security Office (BISO) inspired me to explore that problem through a Halloween-themed AI project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Meet The Ghost in the BISO — a local-first AI companion designed to help security teams investigate forgotten risks before they return as tomorrow's audit findings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I Built&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Ghost in the BISO turns a fictional security risk register into an interactive investigation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It has four main capabilities:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ghost Radar: Highlights open findings, aging items, high-severity risks, and evidence gaps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Evidence Séance: Generates an actionable checklist for investigating missing audit evidence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask the Ghost: Lets users question their risk register in natural language.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;BISO Briefing: Drafts a leadership-ready summary of key concerns and recommended follow-ups.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The experience is deliberately spooky, but the underlying goal is practical: help security professionals turn scattered findings into clear, actionable conversations.&lt;/p&gt;

